{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789214494,"nonce":"g1jGI9/PtaFUMJ6xcp3TjfyUnWQ=","title":"Arbitrary-precision int as an opt-in stdlib type","message":"Python's int is unbounded; the widest primitive is I128. The typed\nsubset defaults `int` to i64, which is what Codon does and what every\nbenchmark that matters expects, with overflow behaviour documented as\nwrapping or trapping rather than promoting.\n\nA `bigint` stdlib type covers the rest, in-language over a List of\nlimbs for the same reason dict is in-language: behind FFI every\narithmetic op is a barrier. Not needed for the first three frontend\nmilestones.","files":null}]}